.TH IPC_RECV 2
.SH NAME
ipc_recv \- wait for a message on a port (SYS_RECV)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long ipc_recv(long port, msg_t *m);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ipc_recv
takes the next message sent to
.IR port ,
which must belong to the calling process, sleeping until one comes.
Several threads of a server may wait on one port: each message goes to
one of them.
.PP
It fills
.IR m\->w[] ,
copies the data into
.I m\->rbuf
(at most
.I m\->rlen
bytes; the rest is cut) and sets
.I m\->rlen
to the size received. The kernel stamps
.IR m\->pid ,
.I m\->uid
and
.I m\->gid
with the sender's identity: a server can trust them to decide what the
caller may do (its other groups:
.BR sys_ident (2)).
.PP
Interrupts bound to the port
.RB ( irq_bind (2))
and kernel events arrive as a message from "token 0": the return value is
0,
.I w[0]
has one bit per interrupt that occurred
.RB ( IRQ_BIT ),
.I w[1]
the other events:
.B NOTE_CHILD
(a child ended,
.BR sys_child_port (2))
and
.B NOTE_WANT
(an on-demand server is wanted,
.BR sys_want (2)).
So a driver needs no extra thread: it waits for clients and hardware in
the same loop.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The sender's token, to answer with
.BR ipc_reply (2);
0 for interrupts and kernel events; or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
No such port, or it is not the caller's.
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal the process catches came while it waited.
.TP
.B EPIPE
The process is ending.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I m
or its
.I rbuf
is not the caller's memory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ipc_reply_recv (2),
.BR ipc_call (2),
.BR ipc_reply (2),
.BR irq_bind (2),
.BR ipc (7)
